e about Britain was manufactured through repressing cultural go-betweens such as Li Zibiao and George Thomas Staunton.","confidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":64,"confidence":0.84},"profileConfidence":0.96,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Chengde","confidence":0.72}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"0f47e5a90bb85d58161a66e76df8fe571df8aca9136b0518a6637fb6ed14bae1","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_figures_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"World History","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores historical interactions between Qing China and the British Empire through a political lens, focusing on the roles of interpreters in these exchanges.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"dfcbfea52aba8734f1194b1bf22a0f4ebc9c95860728c2129cd7049f92b07b77","summary":"A fascinating history of China’s relations with the West—told through the lives of two eighteenth-century translators The 1793 British embassy to China, which led to Lord George Macartney’s fraught encounter with the Qianlong emperor, has often been viewed as a clash of cultures fueled by the East’s lack of interest in the West. In The Perils of Interpreting, Henrietta Harrison presents a more nuanced picture, ingeniously shifting the historical lens to focus on Macartney’s two interpreters at that meeting—Li Zibiao and George Thomas Staunton. Who were these two men? How did they intervene in the exchanges that they mediated? And what did these exchanges mean for them? From Galway to Chengde, and from political intrigues to personal encounters, Harrison reassesses a pivotal moment in relations between China and Britain. She shows that there were Chinese who were familiar with the West, but growing tensions endangered those who embraced both cultures and would eventually culminate in the Opium Wars. Harrison demonstrates that the Qing court’s ignorance about the British did not simply happen, but was manufactured through the repression of cultural go-betweens like Li and Staunton. She traces Li’s influence as Macartney’s interpreter, the pressures Li faced in China as a result, and his later years in hiding.","pageCount":360,"publicationYear":2021,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-the-perils-of-interpreting-the-extraordinary-lives-of-two-translators-between-qing-china-and-the"}],"displaySummary":"A fascinating history of China’s relations with the West—told through the lives of two eighteenth-century translators The 1793 British embassy to China, which led to Lord George Macartney’s fraught encounter with the Qianlong emperor, has often been viewed as a clash of cultures fueled by the East’s lack of interest in the West. In The Perils of Interpreting, Henrietta Harrison presents a more nuanced picture, ingeniously shifting the historical lens to focus on Macartney’s two interpreters at that meeting—Li Zibiao and George Thomas Staunton. Who were these two men? How did they intervene in the exchanges that they mediated? And what did these exchanges mean for them? From Galway to Chengde, and from political intrigues to personal encounters, Harrison reassesses a pivotal moment in relations between China and Britain. She shows that there were Chinese who were familiar with the West, but growing tensions endangered those who embraced both cultures and would eventually culminate in the Opium Wars. Harrison demonstrates that the Qing court’s ignorance about the British did not simply happen, but was manufactured through the repression of cultural go-betweens like Li and Staunton. She traces Li’s influence as Macartney’s interpreter, the pressures Li faced in China as a result, and his later years in hiding.","primaryTopic":"Travel, Exploration & Place","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"World History",